d": "step_15", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_05", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_06", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_02", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "With the prescribed 5000-unit half-window, the best candidate change point (t=81322) shows only 0.268 turnover, i.e. the before/after contact sets overlap ~73% (step_01). The full series never exceeds 0.27 and drifts smoothly between 0.12 and 0.27 with no step discontinuity; widening to 7500 gives the same picture (peak 0.318, step_13). Comparing the extreme early and late thirds, the external contact pool is virtually identical (58, 138, 247, 383, 393, 402, 424, 429, 613, 686, 721, 794, 861, 879, 895, 928, 944, 959, 970, 1014, 1032, 1071, 1136, 1283, 1291, 1605, 1713, 1757, 1761, 1999 appear in both), so there is no old-set/new-set replacement.\n\nThe large n_added/n_removed and jaccard=0.0 in step_03/step_14 are an artifact of edge identity, not partner turnover: the removed and added edges pair the SAME external partners with a DIFFERENT group member (e.g. 826->{58,138,247,...} removed while 1034->{58,138,247,...} and 1641->{58,138,...} added). Confirmed by step_07: members 826/1034/59/610 have strongly anti-phased activity (92, 91, 1, 58, 33, 92 per bin), and 1034 has only a single neighbour early on (step_10). This is round-robin rotation of the emitting member over a fixed audience.\n\nConfounder controls: a comparison group of the partner nodes themselves shows 0.9-1.0 turnover (step_08), so the review group's 0.12-0.27 is far below background - the metric is capable of registering switches here. The partner pool is continuously active in every bin (step_15), so the group's contacts do not disappear and get replaced. Activity is stationary with no bursts (step_02, step_06) and all twelve members have near-identical degree/volume (step_05), ruling out volume or popularity artifacts. The transient 0.60-0.65 spikes at bandwidth 2500 (step_09) coincide with a near-empty stretch - only 15-16 events in [69719,72218] and zero in [71642,72283] versus ~90-104 in neighbouring bins (step_11) - so they are sparsity artifacts, not a transition."}, "raw_final_response": "**Conclusion: no regime switch.** The group's *contact set* is essentially invariant; what rotates is which group member is active against a fixed partner pool.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_8155967f\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [59, 83, 325, 610, 617, 642, 743, 826, 881, 991, 1034, 1641],\n  \"window\": [56322, 86322],\n  \"confidence\": 0.83,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"With the prescribed 5000-unit half-window, the best candidate change point (t=81322) shows only 0.268 turnover, i.e. the before/after contact sets overlap ~73% (step_01). The full series never exceeds 0.27 and drifts smoothly between 0.12 and 0.27 with no step discontinuity; widening to 7500 gives the same picture (peak 0.318, step_13). Comparing the extreme early and late thirds, the external contact pool is virtually identical (58, 138, 247, 383, 393, 402, 424, 429, 613, 686, 721, 794, 861, 879, 895, 928, 944, 959, 970, 1014, 1032, 1071, 1136, 1283, 1291, 1605, 1713, 1757, 1761, 1999 appear in both), so there is no old-set/new-set replacement.\\n\\nThe large n_added/n_removed and jaccard=0.0 in step_03/step_14 are an artifact of edge identity, not partner turnover: the removed and added edges pair the SAME external partners with a DIFFERENT group member (e.g. 826->{58,138,247,...} removed while 1034->{58,138,247,...} and 1641->{58,138,...} added). Confirmed by step_07: members 826/1034/59/610 have strongly anti-phased activity (92, 91, 1, 58, 33, 92 per bin), and 1034 has only a single neighbour early on (step_10). This is round-robin rotation of the emitting member over a fixed audience.\\n\\nConfounder controls: a comparison group of the partner nodes themselves shows 0.9-1.0 turnover (step_08), so the review group's 0.12-0.27 is far below background - the metric is capable of registering switches here. The partner pool is continuously active in every bin (step_15), so the group's contacts do not disappear and get replaced. Activity is stationary with no bursts (step_02, step_06) and all twelve members have near-identical degree/volume (step_05), ruling out volume or popularity artifacts. The transient 0.60-0.65 spikes at bandwidth 2500 (step_09) coincide with a near-empty stretch - only 15-16 events in [69719,72218] and zero in [71642,72283] versus ~90-104 in neighbouring bins (step_11) - so they are sparsity artifacts, not a transition.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 86886, "total_output_tokens": 7043, "total_tokens": 93929, "total_latency_ms": 105422, "llm_calls": 9, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}